  "body": "The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the string '```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}'. Reply B is the string '{\"c\":3}'. For Reply A the rule requires the object {\"b\":2}, because a code fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {\"b\":2}. For Reply B the rule requires the object {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"c\":3}. The check returns {\"c\":3} for both: in Reply A the fenced candidate fails to parse as a single object, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and scores the later {\"c\":3}. The observation that would show this conjecture false is recover_json_object(Reply A) returning {\"b\":2} (or any object other than {\"c\":3}) while recover_json_object(Reply B) returns {\"c\":3}.",
